> vague? Or is it like "ri" and strictly referring to the most recent n>2
> sumti?
>
>
> I use it like "ra" but referring to multiple sumti. I haven't seen this
> word used much, if at all, so I don't know how other people use it. I do
> think, however, that (if understood to work like ra) it is apparent that it
> refers to lung and liver, because the cook is unlikely to cook the queen
> and himself. :)
> If xai is supposed to refer to the last <1 sumti immediately preceeding
> it, then another cmavo would be useful to have that behaves the way I used
> xai in the story (maybe xau). Let's hear what its inventor (Pierre, I
> believe) thinks about it.
>
>
>  lu .i mu'i bo na djica lo nu xaksu ro da fa ny po'o li'u zo'u mi
